      "unless they had seen the evidence Section Three: -Reading Passage II: DIRECTION III: Read the following passage carefully and answer question 51-60 according to the information given below. Understanding the Universe The Universe is the vast totality of space, time, matter, and energy that exists. It includes everything from the smallest subatomic particles to the largest clusters of galaxies. Scientists estimate that the Universe is approximately 13.8 billion years old, having originated from a rapid expansion known as the Big Bang. Since that moment, the Universe has continued to expand, carrying galaxies farther apart over time. One of the most significant discoveries in modern astronomy is that the Universe is not static. Observations made by astronomers such as Edwin Hubble showed that distant galaxies are moving away from Earth, indicating that space itself is expanding. This expansion supports the Big Bang theory and challenges earlier beliefs that the Universe was unchanging. The rate of YEKA SUBCITY EDUCATION OFFICE FIRST SEMESTER ENGLISH MODEL EXAMINATION TIR 2018/JAN 2026 expansion is influenced by mysterious forces, including dark energy, which scientists are still working to fully understand. The Universe is composed of both visible and invisible matter. Ordinary matter, which makes up stars, planets, and living organisms, accounts for only about five percent of the Universe. Dark matter, an unseen substance that does not emit light, makes up roughly twenty-seven percent. Although it cannot be directly observed, dark matter‟s presence is inferred from its gravitational effects on galaxies. The remaining portion of the Universe consists of dark energy, which drives cosmic expansion. Galaxies are massive systems of stars, gas, dust, and dark matter bound together by gravity. Our home, the Milky Way galaxy, contains billions of stars, including the Sun. Galaxies come in various shapes, such as spiral, elliptical, and irregular. They often form clusters and superclusters, demonstrating that matter in the Universe is organized on a vast scale rather than evenly distributed. The study of the Universe helps humanity understand its origins and future. Through advanced telescopes, satellites, and space probes, scientists gather information about distant celestial objects and cosmic events. This knowledge not only deepens scientific understanding but also raises philosophical questions about humanity‟s place in the cosmos. As technology advances, exploration of the Universe continues to reveal new mysteries and possibilities. (Source: Adapted from General Astronomy Knowledge based on NASA AND Encyclopedia Britannica)"
